## GOTCHAS

1. **`FieldSchema` is built with fully-named struct literals in at least 3 places** — adding a field requires updating ALL of them or compilation breaks:
   - `schemars_adapter.rs::field_schema` (lines 226–253)
   - `common/oscal.rs::ensure_category_metadata_fields` → `string_field` closure (lines 70–79)
   - `plugin_schema_defs.rs::field_from_value` (lines ~90–101)
   - Plus any other `FieldSchema { .. }` across plugins — grep `FieldSchema {` before committing. Prefer adding `#[serde(default)] pub <field>` + consider `..Default::default()` only if `FieldSchema` derives `Default` (it does NOT currently derive `Default` — it derives `Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize, PartialEq`).

2. **`FieldSchema` derives `PartialEq`** and is compared field-by-field in the test-only `schema_diffs`/`field_diffs` (schemars_adapter.rs). A new field participates in `PartialEq` automatically, but `field_diffs` will NOT report a difference on your new field unless you add a comparison branch. The `derived_schema_matches_hand_rolled_for_unix_socket` test compares hand-rolled vs derived schemas with `assert!(diffs.is_empty())` — if hand-rolled and derived disagree on the new field's value, `PartialEq` may still pass `schema_diffs` (which only checks listed attributes) but other equality-based tests could fail. Verify the unix_socket round-trip test still passes.

3. **`ensure_category_metadata_fields` vs subid insertion ordering:** In `unix_socket_schema_derived` (plugin_schema_defs.rs), `ensure_category_metadata_fields` is called at line 108 **BEFORE** the `createunixsocket` `mut.*` subid is inserted at line 112. This means the `mut` category is NOT yet present when `ensure_*` runs, so `actor_id`/`capability_id` are NOT auto-added by that call for the `createunixsocket` action. If your new plugin declares a `mut.*` action subid and relies on `ensure_category_metadata_fields` to inject `actor_id`/`capability_id`, you MUST insert the subid FIRST, then call `ensure_category_metadata_fields` last (the `cozo.rs` plugin's `schema()` method calls `ensure_*` after `cozo_schema()` builds all subids — that ordering is correct). The CI test `all_plugin_subids_are_valid_and_unique` (default_registry.rs) enforces that any `mut.*` subid has `actor_id` AND `capability_id` present, any `evt.*` has `event_id` or `event_hash`, any `src.*` has `source_system`+`source_locator`. **Getting the order wrong = CI failure.**

4. **CI subid gates** (default_registry.rs `all_plugin_subids_are_valid_and_unique`): every subid must match `SUBID_REGEX` (oscal.rs:18) AND be globally unique across all plugins. Use a unique subject namespace (e.g. `rocksdb`) and validate format `<category>.<component-type>.<subject>.<verb>[.<facet>][@vN]`. Component-type must be from the fixed list in the regex (`software`, `service`, `network`, etc.).

5. **`__schema__` is the reserved subids key** for the schema-level subid (set from root `x-oscal-subid` or inserted manually). Don't use it as a field/action name.

6. **schemars extension wiring:** For struct-derived schemas, `x-oscal-subid` reaches the adapter via `#[schemars(extend("x-oscal-subid" = "..."))]` on fields/struct. A new `x-embed-content` extension would need the same `#[schemars(extend("x-embed-content" = ...))]` annotation on the struct field AND a read in the `field_schema` (or the property loop) of `schemars_adapter.rs`. Plugins using the `schema_from_state` path (like `cozo`) do NOT go through schemars at all — they build `FieldSchema` via `field_from_value` and insert subids manually, so they'd need a separate mechanism to set any new `FieldSchema` field.

7. **Two distinct schema-build paths exist:** (a) schemars-derived (`plugin_schema_from_json`, used by `unix_socket`), and (b) state-inferred (`schema_from_state` + `field_from_value`, used by `cozo`/`qdrant`). A new `FieldSchema` field must be handled in BOTH if you want both plugin styles to populate it.

8. No `methods`/`MethodSchema` concept exists — do not invent one; model callable operations as `mut.*` (or appropriate category) subid entries keyed by action name, matching `createunixsocket`.

